+  - id: TACTIC:autonomous-operation-protocol
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Autonomous Operation Protocol
+    summary: Enable agents to operate autonomously with clear decision boundaries, commit frequency expectations, and escalation protocols for critical choices.
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-autonomous-operation-protocol.md
+  - id: TACTIC:avoid-gold-plating
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Avoid Gold Plating
+    summary: "Challenge a proposed implementation or design for unnecessary complexity by asking whether each abstraction or feature addresses a current, observed need. Keeps implementation effort proportional to scope, reduces cyclic complexity, and guards against speculative over-engineering that wastes time and calcifies into technical debt.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-avoid-gold-plating.md
+  - id: TACTIC:behavior-driven-development
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Behavior-Driven Development
+    summary: "Define system behavior through concrete examples before implementation, ensuring shared understanding between stakeholders and implementers. BDD scenarios serve as behavioral contracts, acceptance criteria, and executable test definitions simultaneously. Apply when: a feature or business rule is being defined and stakeholders are available, the behavior can be expressed in observable outcomes, or behavioral ambiguity must be resolved before implementation begins. Not applicable: implementation-only tasks with no behavioral ambiguity, purely technical refactoring, or when behavior is already unambiguously specified.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-behavior-driven-development.md
+  - id: TACTIC:black-box-integration-testing
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Black-Box Integration Testing
+    summary: "Validate the functionality of an application under test without peering into its internal structures or workings, by driving it through an external interface and asserting on observable outputs. Tests are derived from external descriptions of the software — specifications, requirements, and design parameters — rather than from its code. \"Black-box\" describes the testing technique (no structural knowledge of the system under test), not the size of the slice under test. Applied here at the integration boundary — exercising more than one collaborator together through a single external interface — but the same discipline also applies at the module, acceptance, and system levels.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-black-box-integration-testing.md
+  - id: TACTIC:boring-code-review
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Boring Code Review
+    summary: "Keep implementation choices predictable, idiomatic, and easy to inspect. Challenge clever control flow, needless abstraction, and DRY violations that duplicate business meaning rather than harmless syntax.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-boring-code-review.md
+  - id: TACTIC:bounded-context-canvas-fill
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Bounded Context Canvas Fill
+    summary: "Guide the reasoning process for completing a Bounded Context Canvas -- a structured one-page summary that captures a context's name, purpose, strategic classification, domain roles, communication patterns, ubiquitous language, business decisions, assumptions, verification metrics, and open questions. This tactic focuses on the analytical thinking required to fill each section well; the fillable canvas itself is a separate template. Based on the DDD Crew Bounded Context Canvas v5.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-bounded-context-canvas-fill.md
+  - id: TACTIC:bounded-context-identification
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Bounded Context Identification
+    summary: "Identify and draw boundaries around regions of a system where a single consistent domain model and ubiquitous language apply. Instead of forcing one unified model across an entire organization, accept that different contexts need different models and define explicit integration points between them. Based on Eric Evans's DDD strategic design and Martin Fowler's Bounded Context pattern.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-bounded-context-identification.md
+  - id: TACTIC:bug-fixing-checklist
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Test-First Bug Fixing Checklist
+    summary: "Resolve defects through a disciplined sequence that prevents speculative fixes, ensures the defect is reproducible as a test, and verifies no regressions are introduced. Applicable to any language or framework. Do not skip steps under time pressure — shortcuts in bug fixing cause regressions.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-bug-fixing-checklist.md
+  - id: TACTIC:c4-zoom-in-architecture-documentation
+    kind: tactic
+    title: C4 Zoom-In Architecture Documentation
+    summary: "Progressively document a system's architecture by starting at the broadest abstraction (system context) and zooming in only where additional detail adds value. Each level targets a specific audience and answers a specific question, preventing the common failure of a single monolithic diagram that serves nobody well. Based on Simon Brown's C4 model (c4model.com).\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-c4-zoom-in-architecture-documentation.md
+  - id: TACTIC:canonical-source-unification
+    kind: tactic
+    title: canonical-source-unification
+    summary: Definition unavailable in bundled doctrine.
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-canonical-source-unification.md
+  - id: TACTIC:chain-of-responsibility-rule-pipeline
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Chain of Responsibility — Rule-Based Pipeline
+    summary: "Express a complex multi-step rule application as a pipeline of small, pure, independently-testable functions instead of as one large branching function. Each rule decides on its own applicability, executes its narrow piece of work, and returns a result object that the runner threads to the next rule. The pattern keeps cognitive complexity per function low, makes the rule set readable as a sequence, and lets new rules slot in without re-reading the whole pipeline.\nThis is the codebase's accepted way of dealing with stacks of related but independent rules — validators, classifiers, canonicalizers, scorers, audit detectors. The shape recurs in `src/method/audit/`, `src/method/charter_runtime/lint/checks/`, `src/method/migration/`, and `src/doctrine/agent_profiles/repository.py` (DDR-011 scoring). New code that composes 4+ independent decisions over the same input should reach for this pattern by default.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-chain-of-responsibility-rule-pipeline.md
