+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-compositional-stream-boundaries.md
+  - id: TACTIC:connascence-analysis
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Connascence Analysis
+    summary: "Diagnose coupling between software components by classifying it using the connascence taxonomy — examining strength, degree, and locality — and recommend targeted reductions that improve maintainability without unnecessary abstraction.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-connascence-analysis.md
+  - id: TACTIC:context-boundary-inference
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Context Boundary Inference
+    summary: "Detect bounded context boundaries from team ownership patterns and terminology conflicts. Use systematic organizational and linguistic analysis to surface implicit domain boundaries before they calcify into accidental coupling. Apply when: a monolith is growing beyond a single team's cognitive bandwidth, teams disagree on what a shared term means, cross-team coupling makes deployment or testing painful, or a strategic design effort is beginning. Success criteria: each proposed context has explicit ownership, ubiquitous language documented per context, translation rules defined at each boundary, no shared mutable state across context boundaries, proposed boundaries validated with domain experts.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-context-boundary-inference.md
+  - id: TACTIC:context-mapping-classification
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Context Mapping Classification
+    summary: "Systematically classify every relationship between bounded contexts using the nine canonical DDD context mapping patterns and three relationship types. Produces an explicit map that reveals coupling, power asymmetries, and integration risk across the system. Based on Eric Evans's strategic design patterns and the DDD Crew context-mapping notation.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-context-mapping-classification.md
+  - id: TACTIC:cross-cutting-state-via-store
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Cross-Cutting State via Store
+    summary: "Use a centralized store (Redux, NgRx, Zustand, Pinia) exclusively for state that crosses atomic boundaries: auth, theme, locale, feature flags, and domain entities consumed by multiple organisms on different pages. Keeps the store lean and purposeful.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-cross-cutting-state-via-store.md
+  - id: TACTIC:decision-marker-capture
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Decision Marker Capture
+    summary: Ensure architectural decisions made during implementation are captured as traceable records with rationale, alternatives, and consequences, so future maintainers can understand why a choice was made.
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-decision-marker-capture.md
+  - id: TACTIC:deepening-opportunity-assessment
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Deepening Opportunity Assessment
+    summary: "Identify whether a shallow cluster of modules should be deepened into a higher-leverage module with a smaller, more stable interface. The assessment focuses on real caller friction, test seam quality, dependency shape, and locality of future changes rather than on abstract cleanliness.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-deepening-opportunity-assessment.md
+  - id: TACTIC:delete-the-assertion-not-the-test
+    kind: tactic
+    title: delete-the-assertion-not-the-test
+    summary: Definition unavailable in bundled doctrine.
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-delete-the-assertion-not-the-test.md
+  - id: TACTIC:dependency-hygiene
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Dependency Hygiene
+    summary: "Keep a project's dependency tree free of known vulnerabilities and non-compliant licenses. Apply at project creation, when adding any dependency, and as part of regular pre-release maintenance. Apply when: starting a new project, adding or upgrading any dependency, a vulnerability scanner reports new CVEs, or preparing a release. For JavaScript/TypeScript projects specifically, dependency hygiene also covers install-time supply-chain safety (registry authenticity, package freshness, lifecycle-script discipline, lockfile behavior, and Node runtime baseline) per the supply-chain-install-safety directive and tactic — see the JS/TS-specific steps below.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-dependency-hygiene.md
+  - id: TACTIC:development-bdd
+    kind: tactic
+    title: BDD as Behavioral Contract Design
+    summary: "Use Behaviour-Driven Development principles to define observable behavioral contracts before implementation begins, ensuring system boundaries are expressed in terms stakeholders can validate. Apply during design to establish what the system must do at its public interfaces — not how to write test scenarios (see the behavior-driven-development tactic for that). Outputs of this tactic serve as the behavioral contract that step definitions later implement.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-development-bdd.md
+  - id: TACTIC:documentation-curation-audit
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Documentation Curation Audit
+    summary: Systematically audit a documentation directory for structural consistency, naming conventions, cross-reference integrity, and metadata completeness.
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-documentation-curation-audit.md
+  - id: TACTIC:domain-event-capture
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Domain Event Capture
+    summary: "Funnel significant state changes through immutable Domain Event objects that are persisted before processing. Separates input recording from business reaction, enabling auditability, replay, and decoupled system evolution. Based on Martin Fowler's Domain Event pattern and Eric Evans's DDD events.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-domain-event-capture.md
+  - id: TACTIC:easy-to-change
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Easy-to-Change Design Review
+    summary: "Assess whether a proposed or existing design keeps the cost of future change proportional to the value gained. Identifies where assumptions are embedded in the wrong places, flags premature abstraction, and recommends minimal structural investments that preserve adaptability without over-engineering.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-easy-to-change.md
+  - id: TACTIC:eisenhower-prioritisation
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Eisenhower Prioritisation
+    summary: "Assign each pending task an importance and urgency rating so that the most valuable work is tackled first and low-value work is explicitly eliminated or delegated. Prevents constant busyness from masking a lack of meaningful progress.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-eisenhower-prioritisation.md
+  - id: TACTIC:entity-value-object-classification
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Entity vs Value Object Classification
+    summary: "Classify every domain object as either an Entity (has identity, mutable lifecycle) or a Value Object (defined solely by its attributes, immutable, no identity). The default heuristic is to prefer Value Objects; promote to Entity only when the domain requires tracking identity across state changes. Correct classification reduces complexity, improves testability, and makes the domain model more expressive. Based on Eric Evans's DDD tactical patterns.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-entity-value-object-classification.md
+  - id: TACTIC:five-paradigm-parallel-debugging
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Five-Paradigm Parallel Debugging
+    summary: "Investigate stubborn, recurring, or shape-shifting bugs by dispatching five sub-agents in parallel, each constrained to a different debugging epistemology. Independent investigations converge on a single structural fix while each surfaces findings the others structurally cannot. Produces one structural intervention, not five point-fixes.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-five-paradigm-parallel-debugging.md
+  - id: TACTIC:focused-function-complexity-check
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Focused Function Complexity Check
+    summary: "Keep functions focused, readable, and bounded in complexity without splitting behavior into needless fragments. The goal is clear units of behavior whose control flow can be inspected quickly.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-focused-function-complexity-check.md
+  - id: TACTIC:forensic-repository-audit
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Forensic Repository Audit
+    summary: "Extract durable, evidence-backed signals about the health of an unfamiliar codebase from version control history and surface them as concrete questions a maintainer must answer: where is risk concentrated, who knows this code, is the project alive, does the team trust the pipeline, is anything abandoned, and is complexity load-bearing? The output is not a verdict but a structured inventory of hotspots, ownership, velocity, and firefighting frequency that other doctrine artifacts (triage, prioritisation, premortem, connascence analysis) can act on.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-forensic-repository-audit.md
+  - id: TACTIC:formalized-constraint-testing
+    kind: tactic
+    title: Formalized Constraint Testing
+    summary: "Verify mathematical invariants and structural contracts of components using property-based patterns rather than example-based spot-checks. Covers four pattern families: round-trip symmetry, equality contracts, hash-code alignment, and sparse serialization.\n"
+    source_path: ''
+    local_path: _LIBRARY/tactic-formalized-constraint-testing.md
